// The walking skeleton: the smallest program that drives every crate, and the
// one `crates/fossil-cli/tests/walking_skeleton.rs` asserts by content — five
// `Person` vertices, subjects `https://example.org/user/1`…`/5`, and the five
// names from `users.csv` on the `name` column.
//
// Every path here is relative to THIS FILE and not to wherever you invoked
// `fossil` from: `users.csv` and `hello.shex` sit beside it in `examples/`. The
// source read `examples/users.csv`, which only resolved because the engine fell
// back to the process's working directory when the program-relative path did
// not exist — so this program meant a different file depending on where you ran
// it, and meant nothing at all from anywhere but the repository root.

// The output contract. Since ruling 3 of 2026-08-11 this binding is MANDATORY:
// a property key is the last segment of a predicate IRI the shape declares, so
// without the document `name` below resolves to nothing and the mapping writes
// no properties at all. `hello.shex` declares `ex:name xsd:string` at 1..1.
type { Person } := io.shex("hello.shex")

User := io.csv("users.csv")

Users : Person from User
    // The identity slot (ADR-0058). The hole is an ordinary expression inside
    // an ordinary quoted string — there is one string spelling, and `prefix` +
    // the CURIE it fed are gone from every position they held.
    @subject = "https://example.org/user/{User.id}"
    name     = User.name
